  "body": "Found during the work on #2394 (example case turning/cone-thread).\n\n## What happens\n\nThe Threading operation starts each pass at the top lattice point of the thread. This point is always inside the length of the part. A pass cannot start in front of the end face (see the related issue about the threading verdict).\n\nWith the post option \"Threading: G76 cycle\", the Fanuc lathe post writes one rapid to the start point and then the two G76 blocks. The start Z is the top lattice point.\n\nA Fanuc-type control moves the insert from the start point to the depth of each pass at rapid traverse. It does this at the start Z. At that Z the bar is solid. Thus the control moves the threading insert into the material at rapid traverse, one time for each pass.\n\nThe G32 output of the same thread does not have this problem. It writes the infeed as a `G01` block at the plunge feed.\n\n## Example\n\nThe case turning/cone-thread of #2394, with `set_post_options { \"threading\": \"g76\" }`, posts:\n\n```\nG00 X30.1 Z77.4\nG76 P010000 Q250 R0.17\nG76 X8.16 Z63.9 P920 Q250 F1.5\n```\n\nThe part end face is at Z78. The thread diameter is 10 mm from Z63 to Z78. At Z77.4 the insert (1.2 mm wide) stands above solid material from Z76.8 to Z78.\n\nThe golden `crates/app/tests/golden/nc/fanuc-lathe/thread_g76.nc` has the same shape: `G00 X12.1 Z14.` on a bar that continues at 12 mm diameter to Z40.\n\n## What is expected\n\nA G76 cycle starts in free air: in front of the end face, or in a relief groove that is wider than the insert. If the start point is above material, the post refuses the G76 cycle by name. It does not write the cycle.\n\n## Basis\n\nThe G76 motion (rapid infeed at the start Z, feed along Z, rapid retract, rapid return) is from the public Fanuc lathe programming manuals. No machine ran this program. A person with a Fanuc lathe or simulator must confirm the reading.\n\n## Workaround\n\nUse G32 passes (the default). The case of #2394 does this.\n\nRefs #2394\n",
